public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Coherent.HomColimit.Injectivity
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.DivisorFlatPullback.Basic
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.FlatBaseChange.GlobalGeneration
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.TwoGeneratorSquare

/-!
# The inverse image of the centre of the two-generator square is generated by two sections

For `f₁, f₂` in a commutative ring `A`, the two-generator square of
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/TwoGeneratorSquare.lean` has modification
`b : X' = Proj (A[T₀,T₁]/(f₂T₀-f₁T₁)) ⟶ X = Spec A` and centre `Z = Spec (A/(f₁,f₂))`. The ideal
sheaf of the centre is the one attached to `(f₁,f₂)` on `Spec A`, so it is generated by the two
global sections `f₁` and `f₂`; this file carries that through the inverse image, proving that the
sheaf of ideals `ℐ = b^{-1}ℐ_Z` of the exceptional divisor on the modification is generated by
the two global sections of `𝒪_{X'}` pulled back from `f₁` and `f₂`.

That is the one remaining geometric input to step (b) of [Stacks, Tag 0EXD].

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.span_germTop_eq_top_of_affineLocal`: the germs of a family of global
  sections of an `𝒪_X`-module span every stalk as soon as, over every affine open, the
  restrictions of the family span the sections there.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.generatingSectionsOfAffineLocal`: consequently such a family is a family of
  generating global sections, [Stacks, Tag 01AN].
* `Stacks.IdealSheaf.ideal_comap_isAffineOpen_eq_span`: on an affine open of the source, the
  inverse image of a sheaf of ideals of an affine scheme is generated by the pullbacks of a set
  of generators of its ideal of global sections.
* `Stacks.IdealSheaf.comapGeneratingSections`: **the inverse image of a sheaf of ideals of an
  affine scheme is generated by the pullbacks of generators of its ideal of global sections.**
* `Stacks.AlmostBlowUp.generatingSections_comap_centerIdealSheaf`: **the ideal sheaf of the
  exceptional divisor of the two-generator square is generated by the two global sections `f₁`
  and `f₂`.**

## Implementation notes

**Why stalks.** Generation by global sections is the statement that the induced map from a free
sheaf of modules is an epimorphism, and an epimorphism of sheaves of modules is *not* detected on
sections; the available criterion is the stalkwise one,
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.FlatBaseChange.generates_iff_span_germTop_eq_top` (Tag 01AN). The
hypothesis actually available here is affine-local — over an affine open the sections of the
inverse image ideal sheaf really are spanned by the restrictions of the two pullbacks — and the
passage from it to the stalk is the shrinking argument in
`AlgebraicGeometry.span_germTop_eq_top_of_affineLocal`: an element of the stalk is the germ of a
section over some open, which may be restricted to an affine open around the point before the
hypothesis is applied. `Stacks.Duality.span_germ_of_forall_eq_sum` is the same argument without
the shrinking, so its hypothesis — a global combination over *every* open — is one an ideal
sheaf does not satisfy.

**Two spellings of the sections of a module on a scheme.** `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Modules` is
a definition for `SheafOfModules X.ringCatSheaf`, and the scalars of a section module are
presented both as `Γ(X, V)` in `CommRingCat` and as `X.ringCatSheaf.obj.obj (op V)` in `RingCat`;
the `Module` instances over the two are the same instance by `rfl` but typeclass resolution does
not cross between them. Every step that would otherwise be a rewrite along `map_add`,
`map_zero`, `PresheafOfModules.germ_smul` or `Submodule.map_span` is therefore first stated at
the spelling the goal carries and only then rewritten — `hadd`, `hzero` and `hsmul` inside
`span_germTop_eq_top_of_affineLocal` — and the comparison of a submodule of the structure sheaf
with the ideal it is over, where neither spelling of `Submodule.map` applies, is done by hand
with `Submodule.span_induction` in
`Stacks.IdealSheaf.span_restrict_idealPowOne_comap_eq_top`.

**The encoding of the ideal sheaf as a module.** The first power
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.IdealPowerColimit.idealPow K 1` is used, whose sections over an affine
open `V` are the ideal `K(V)` by
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.IdealPowerColimit.obj_idealPow_affineOpen`; that computation is the
only property of the encoding that is needed, and it is what makes the affine-local hypothesis
checkable.

No `@[stacks]` attribute: this is a step inside the verification of the almost blowup square of
[Stacks, Tag 0EVH], not a statement of the source.

